"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1623,"Narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdah, narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, informed us Abu Hazim, and it was narrated to us Ya‘qub ibn Ibraheem ad-Dawraqi, narrated to us ‘Abd al-‘Aziz ibn Abi Hazim, from his father, and it was narrated to us al-‘Abbar ibn al-‘Ala’, narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard Abu Hazim narrate from Sahl ibn Sa‘d.

And it was narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la as-Sadafi, informed us ‘Abdullah ibn Wahb, that Malik informed him, from Abu Hazim ibn Dinar, from Sahl ibn Sa‘d, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out to the Banu ‘Amr ibn ‘Awf to reconcile between them. The time for prayer came, and the caller to prayer came to Abu Bakr and said: “Will you lead the people in prayer so that I may give the iqamah?” He said: “Yes,” so Abu Bakr led the people in prayer.

Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came while the people were in prayer, so he slipped through until he stood in the row. The people clapped, and Abu Bakr did not turn around in his prayer. When the people clapped a great deal, he turned and saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gestured to him to remain in your place. So Abu Bakr raised his hands and praised Allah for what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had commanded him to do. Then Abu Bakr stepped back until he aligned himself in the row, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stepped forward and led the prayer.

When he finished, he (ﷺ) said: “O Abu Bakr! What prevented you from remaining in your place when I commanded you to do so?”

Abu Bakr said: “It was not for the son of Abu Quhafah to pray in front of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).”

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “What is the matter with you that I saw you clapping so much? Whoever is distracted in his prayer should say Subhanallah, for if he says Subhanallah, he will be turned to. Clapping is only for women.”

This is the narration of Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la.

Abu Bakr said: In this report there is evidence that if the one praying is addressed with Subhanallah, it is permissible for him to turn to the one saying it in order to know what distracted the speaker so that he may do what is required of him.

---

**CHAPTER:**"
